Z||000000||porridgeman||05-07-2001||07:16 PM||||hi everyone, this weekend i am going to tackle DOOR HINGE PINS & BUSHINGS!i have read the info on the past forums. My question is : can anyone direct me to a picture of the tool that a guy will need for compressing the spring on the door. I could probably make one if i see a picture of it.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000001||qwikgta||05-07-2001||10:06 PM||||Maybe Im wrong, but I changed both the top and bottom pins and bushings in my 88 coupe and all i used was a floor jack under the door.  I looked in, saw the bushing in two pieces and bought new ones.  Jacked up the door to keep pressure off and tapped the old ones out/put new ones in.  As I recall it was not to difficult, just took time.  Never read "how to" and never needed a speciel tool.  Just my $.02<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||5
Z||000002||mbramble||05-07-2001||10:29 PM||||Welcome to the forum...<P>Check the Ogre's Cave link at the bottom of this page.  Go to the Door Service link in the Body & Chasis section.  Besides good how-to info, there is a link to show you the tool, I believe.<P>Not having such a tool, I used a 3/8" all thread bolt that was just a bit longer than the uncompressed spring.  I put a large washer on each end of the spring to keep the bolt from slipping thru and then tightened a nut until the spring was completely compressed.  I then fished a piece of wire down thru the spring and twisted it tight with a pair of pliers (tap the washers over to one side of the spring to provide a little space to fit the wire down the inside of the spring).  I broke several pieces of wire before I got it tight without breaking.  I then removed the bolt and the spring stayed compressed.  It will try to expand since the wire is only holding it on one side but it holds well enough to slip the spring back into place.  I then used a very large C clamp to hold the spring in place against the door and hinge while I cut the wire.  The hardest part of the whole thing was getting the cut wire out!!<P>I now keep this bolt, washers, and nut in my tool bag and was able to put the spring back onto my S-10 door, after replacing the pin, in about 10 minutes.  ||||reg||1
